# Terraform Variables – Complete Study Notes

## 1. Introduction to Terraform Variables

In Terraform, variables are used to make configurations more dynamic and reusable.

Terraform variables allow us to parameterize configurations instead of hardcoding values directly into configuration files.

**Benefits of Variables:**
- Make code flexible and reusable.
- Avoid hardcoding values.
- Make configurations easier to manage.
- Customize Terraform deployments.

## 2. Types of Terraform Variables

### 1. Input Variables
- Used to pass values into Terraform configurations.
- Defined using the `variable` block.

### 2. Output Variables
- Used to return values from Terraform configurations after applying.
- Help share information between configurations or modules.

### 3. Local Variables
- Used to assign values to expressions.
- Improve code readability and maintainability.

## 3. Terraform Variable Types

| Variable Type | Description |
|---|---|
| string | Sequence of characters |
| number | Numeric values |
| bool | True or false |
| list(type) | Ordered collection of elements |
| map(type) | Key-value pairs |
| set(type) | Unique unordered elements |
| object({...}) | Structured object with named attributes |
| tuple([...]) | Fixed sequence of different types |

## 4. List Functions

### length()
Returns the number of elements in a list.

```hcl
variable "fruits" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
}

output "list_length" {
  value = length(var.fruits)
}
```

### concat()
Combines multiple lists into a single list.

```hcl
variable "fruits" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["apple", "grapes"]
}

variable "vegies" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["onions", "tomatoes"]
}

output "combined_list" {
  value = concat(var.fruits, var.vegies)
}
```

### element()
Returns an element at a specified index.

```hcl
variable "fruits" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
}

output "selected_element" {
  value = element(var.fruits, 1)
}
```

### zipmap()
Creates a map using a list of keys and a list of values.

```hcl
variable "keys" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["name", "age"]
}

variable "values" {
  type    = list(any)
  default = ["Alice", 30]
}

output "my_map" {
  value = zipmap(var.keys, var.values)
}
```

### lookup()
Retrieves a value associated with a specific key in a map.

```hcl
variable "my_map" {
  type = map(string)
  default = {
    name = "Venky"
    age  = "50"
  }
}

output "value" {
  value = lookup(var.my_map, "name")
}
```

### join()
Joins list elements into a single string using a separator.

```hcl
variable "fruits" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
}

output "joined_string" {
  value = join(", ", var.fruits)
}
```

## 5. Set Example

A set is used when uniqueness is required and order does not matter.

```hcl
variable "unique_names" {
  type    = set(string)
  default = ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "Bob"]
}

output "unique_names_list" {
  value = tolist(var.unique_names)
}
```

## 6. Map Example

A map stores data in key-value pairs.

```hcl
variable "instance_tags" {
  type = map(string)

  default = {
    Name = "WebServer"
    Env  = "Production"
  }
}

output "instance_tags_output" {
  value = var.instance_tags
}

output "instance_name" {
  value = var.instance_tags["Name"]
}

output "tag_keys" {
  value = keys(var.instance_tags)
}

output "tag_values" {
  value = values(var.instance_tags)
}
```

## 7. Tuple Example

A tuple allows different data types in a fixed order.

```hcl
variable "mixed_values" {
  type    = tuple([string, number, bool])
  default = ["example", 42, true]
}

output "mixed_values_output" {
  value = var.mixed_values
}

output "first_element" {
  value = var.mixed_values[0]
}

output "second_element" {
  value = var.mixed_values[1]
}

output "third_element" {
  value = var.mixed_values[2]
}
```

## 8. Practical Example – Creating EC2 Instances Using Variables

Create `main.tf`.

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

variable "instance_count" {
  description = "*"
  type        = number
  default     = 3
}

variable "instance_ami" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
  default     = "ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
}

variable "instance_type" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
  default     = "t2.micro"
}

variable "instance_name" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
  default     = "TF-Server"
}

resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  count         = var.instance_count
  ami           = var.instance_ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name = var.instance_name
  }
}
```

### Terraform Commands

```bash
terraform fmt
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
```

## 9. Separating Variables into variables.tf

To manage variables easily, we can move variable definitions into a separate file called `variables.tf`.

Terraform automatically loads `.tf` files in the same working directory.

### main.tf

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  count         = var.instance_count
  ami           = var.instance_ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name = var.instance_name
  }
}
```

### variables.tf

```hcl
variable "instance_count" {
  description = "*"
  type        = number
  default     = 1
}

variable "instance_ami" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
  default     = "ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
}

variable "instance_type" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
  default     = "t2.micro"
}

variable "instance_name" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
  default     = "TF-Server"
}
```

### Commands

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve
terraform state list
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

## 10. Terraform Variables Files – .tfvars

`.tfvars` files allow us to separate variable values from the main Terraform configuration.

They are useful when managing multiple environments such as:

- Development
- Testing
- Production

Terraform automatically recognizes `terraform.tfvars` as a default variable values file.

We can also create custom files:

- `dev.tfvars`
- `test.tfvars`
- `prod.tfvars`

### variables.tf

Remove the default values and keep only the variable definitions.

```hcl
variable "instance_count" {
  type = number
}

variable "instance_ami" {
  type = string
}

variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

variable "instance_name" {
  type = string
}
```

### dev.tfvars

```hcl
instance_count = 1
instance_ami   = "ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
instance_type  = "t2.micro"
instance_name  = "Dev-Server"
```

### prod.tfvars

```hcl
instance_count = 1
instance_ami   = "ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
instance_type  = "t2.micro"
instance_name  = "Prod-Server"
```

### Applying Different Environments

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve -var-file="dev.tfvars"

terraform apply --auto-approve -var-file="test.tfvars"

terraform apply --auto-approve -var-file="prod.tfvars"
```

### Destroying Resources

```bash
terraform destroy --auto-approve -var-file="test.tfvars"

terraform destroy --auto-approve -var-file="prod.tfvars"
```

**Note:** These commands use the default workspace unless a different workspace is selected. Applying different variable files does not automatically create separate environments or state files.

## 11. Command-Line Variables

Terraform allows us to provide variable values directly through command-line arguments.

If variable values are not provided through defaults or variable files, Terraform can ask for input interactively.

### Example

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve
```

### Providing Variables Using -var

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve \
-var="instance_type=t2.micro" \
-var="instance_count=1" \
-var="instance_name=test-server" \
-var="instance_ami=ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
```

### Destroy Command

```bash
terraform destroy --auto-approve \
-var="instance_type=t2.micro" \
-var="instance_count=1" \
-var="instance_name=test-server" \
-var="instance_ami=ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
```

## 12. Environment Variables Using export

Terraform also supports environment variables for providing input variable values.

The environment variable must follow the format:

`TF_VAR_variable_name`

### Example

```bash
export TF_VAR_ami=ami-0492447090ced6eb5
```

### variables.tf

```hcl
variable "ami" {
  description = "*"
  type        = string
}
```

### main.tf

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  count         = 1
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "reya-server"
  }
}
```

### Commands

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

## 13. Terraform Output Variables

Output variables are used to display information about resources after Terraform applies the configuration.

### Example – EC2 Instance Information

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "myinstance" {
  ami           = "ami-0492447090ced6eb5"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "outputvarexample-server"
  }
}

output "instance-information" {
  value = [
    aws_instance.myinstance.public_ip,
    aws_instance.myinstance.private_ip,
    aws_instance.myinstance.public_dns
  ]
}
```

### Apply Command

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve
```

The output will display the EC2 instance information in the command line.

### Getting All EC2 Instance Information

We can also return the complete resource object.

```hcl
output "instance-information" {
  value = aws_instance.myinstance
}
```

### Destroy Command

```bash
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

## 14. Important Terraform Commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `terraform fmt` | Formats Terraform files |
| `terraform plan` | Shows the execution plan |
| `terraform apply` | Applies the configuration |
| `terraform destroy` | Destroys managed resources |
| `terraform state list` | Lists resources in state |
| `terraform workspace list` | Lists available workspaces |
| `terraform apply -var-file="dev.tfvars"` | Applies values from a variable file |

